WB.L("backing-up-your-files", "life", "beginner", "Backing up: never lose your photos again",
  "In 1998, a single mistyped command wiped out most of Pixar's Toy Story 2. What saved the film was a backup nobody knew existed.",
  [
    ["Even experts lose data", "Data is lost through ordinary accidents and failures, even at world-class studios, so it can happen to you.",
      "In 1998, while Pixar was making Toy Story 2, someone ran a command that began deleting the film's files from the shared drives. Characters, sets and animation disappeared as staff watched. The studio's regular backups turned out not to be working properly. The film was rescued because technical director Galyn Susman, working from home after having a baby, had a fairly recent copy on her home computer. It was carefully driven back to the studio and most of the work was restored.\n\nData vanishes in ordinary ways: a phone dropped in the surf, a stolen laptop, a drive that quietly fails, an accidental delete, a fire or flood. Every drive eventually fails. The question is not whether something will go wrong, but whether you will lose anything when it does."],
    ["The 3-2-1 rule", "Keep three copies of what matters, on two kinds of storage, with one copy offsite.",
      "A widely quoted rule of thumb, popularised by photographer Peter Krogh, is 3-2-1. Keep at least three copies of anything important: the original plus two backups. Store them on at least two different kinds of storage, such as your computer's internal drive and an external drive, so one type of failure cannot take them all.\n\nKeep at least one copy offsite, somewhere physically separate, so a fire, flood or burglary at home leaves something behind. Today that is usually a cloud backup service, but a drive kept at a relative's house or your workplace also counts. The rule is not magic, but it makes you think about every way your data could be destroyed at once."],
    ["A copy out of reach", "Sync copies your mistakes everywhere, so keep a backup that mistakes and malware cannot reach.",
      "Many people assume their files are safe in iCloud, Google Drive, OneDrive or Dropbox. Most of these are built mainly to sync, keeping files identical across devices. So a deleted folder or overwritten document can spread to every device within seconds. Many keep deleted files and older versions for a limited time, so learn how long and how to restore them.\n\nRansomware is software that encrypts your files and demands payment, often in cryptocurrency, for the key. Paying guarantees nothing and funds criminals. A drive that is permanently plugged in can be encrypted too. Keep a backup drive unplugged except during backups, or use a cloud service that keeps older versions. The Australian Cyber Security Centre lists regular backups among its essential strategies."],
    ["Phones and photos", "Your phone probably holds your most irreplaceable memories, so check that it really backs itself up.",
      "For many people the irreplaceable files are photos and videos of children, travels and people who are no longer here. Most now live on a phone, which is easy to lose, drop or break. iPhones and Android phones can both back up automatically to the cloud, but the free storage is often small. Once it fills up, backups can silently stop.\n\nCheck your phone's settings to see when it last backed up successfully. Paying a few dollars a month for more storage is cheap insurance. Every so often, download your photo library to a computer or external drive as well, so your memories do not depend on a single company or account login."],
    ["Test it, then automate it", "A backup you have never restored from is only a hope, and the one that runs by itself is the one that happens.",
      "Pixar's backups were running, or so everyone thought, until the day they were needed. Every few months, pick a random file or photo and try to recover it from each backup. Check that it opens. Make sure you know your cloud password and have two-factor authentication sorted, or your backup may be locked behind a login you cannot remember on the day your phone is lost.\n\nNobody remembers to copy files by hand every week, so automate. Turn on automatic phone backups, use Time Machine on a Mac or File History on Windows with an external drive, and add a cloud service for the offsite copy. Start with photos, important documents and tax records. Any backup is infinitely better than none."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the Toy Story 2 story show about backups?", ["Backups are only needed for large companies","Even a top studio can come close to losing everything","Home computers are safer than shared drives","Deleted files can always be recovered easily"], 1, "If a world-class studio nearly lost its film, ordinary people can lose their photos too."],
    [1, "Why does the 3-2-1 rule include an offsite copy?", ["Home disasters such as fire cannot reach it","It speeds up the copying process","Offsite copies never need to be tested","It replaces the need for a second copy"], 0, "A physically separate copy survives disasters that destroy everything kept in one place."],
    [2, "Why is a syncing service not a true backup on its own?", ["It cannot store photos or documents","It only works on one device at a time","It deletes files after a fixed number of days","A deletion or overwrite can spread everywhere"], 3, "Sync keeps devices identical, so mistakes copy everywhere unless older versions are kept."],
    [3, "What can quietly stop a phone's cloud backup from working?", ["Changing the phone's ringtone","Turning the screen off overnight","Updating the phone's apps","Free storage filling up"], 3, "When storage is full, backups can silently stop, so check the date of the last successful backup."],
    [4, "Why should you restore a random file from your backup every few months?", ["To free up space on your drive","To make the next backup run faster","To check it works before you need it","To avoid paying for cloud storage"], 2, "Only a tested restore proves you have the copies you believe you have."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A mistyped command wiped out a Pixar film. Something unexpected saved it."});

WB.L("useful-knots", "life", "beginner", "Six knots worth knowing",
  "Most people know one knot and use it for everything. Sailors, climbers and truckies know better, and a handful of knots will cover almost anything you need.",
  [
    ["Terms and the reef knot", "Learn the basic rope terms, and remember the reef knot suits bandages and parcels but not joining ropes under load.",
      "Knot instructions use a small vocabulary. The working end is the end you move to tie the knot. The standing part is the rest of the rope, often the part under load. A bight is a U-shaped fold, and a loop is a bight where the rope crosses itself. Almost every knot weakens a rope, so climbing or lifting people needs proper training.\n\nThe reef knot, or square knot, is right over left, then left over right. It lies flat, so it suits bandages, slings and parcels. Get the second step wrong and you have a granny knot, which slips. To join two ropes under strain, a sheet bend is far more secure, because a reef knot can capsize."],
    ["The bowline", "The bowline makes a fixed loop that will not slip and stays easy to untie, even after a heavy load.",
      "Pronounced ‘boh-lin’, the bowline has been called the king of knots. It forms a loop at the end of a rope that does not tighten or slide. That makes it useful for tying a rope around a tree or post, or, in a rescue, a person. Children learn it with a story: make a small loop, then the rabbit (the working end) comes up out of the hole, around the tree and back down the hole.\n\nAfter taking a heavy load, a bowline can still be loosened by pushing the collar. Its weakness is that it can work loose when not under load, so climbers usually prefer a figure-eight loop."],
    ["Tying to a post", "A clove hitch is quick but can slip, while a round turn and two half hitches holds even under strain.",
      "The clove hitch is two turns around a pole, the second crossing over the first and tucked under itself. It is fast to tie and easy to adjust and undo. It secures boat fenders, starts lashings and ties off tent lines and garden stakes. It is not set-and-forget: if the post rotates or the load changes direction, it can work loose. For a long-term tie, add half hitches.\n\nIf a rope is already under tension, perhaps from a boat drifting away, use a round turn and two half hitches. Pass the rope around the object twice, so friction takes most of the load. Then make a half hitch around the standing part, and repeat it in the same direction. It is strong, secure and untiable after a strain. Boaties use it for mooring."],
    ["Stoppers and tensioners", "A figure-eight stops a rope escaping, and a trucker's hitch lets you pull a load down tight.",
      "A stopper knot keeps a rope from escaping. An overhand knot works but can jam tight. The figure-eight is better: make a loop, pass the end around the standing part, then back through the loop. It is bulkier, so it is less likely to pull through, and easier to untie. Sailors use it on lines, and it suits drawstrings and clotheslines. Threaded back along itself, it forms the loop climbers rely on.\n\nThe trucker's hitch works like a simple pulley. Make a fixed loop partway along the rope, pass the working end around an anchor point such as a tie-down hook, then back through the loop. The mechanical advantage is theoretically about three to one, though friction reduces it. Pinch it, secure with two half hitches, and recheck the load after a few kilometres."],
    ["Practise until hands know", "Knots are learned by the hands, not the eyes, so practise until you can tie them without thinking.",
      "Reading about knots is a little like reading about swimming. It only sticks once you have done it many times. Keep a short length of rope near the couch and tie each knot while watching television until you can do it without looking.\n\nThen practise in awkward conditions: with the rope behind a post, in the dark, or with cold hands. That is when you will actually need it, while a tarp flaps in a storm or a boat drifts from the jetty. Start with the bowline, the clove hitch and the round turn with two half hitches. Those three alone will handle most everyday jobs."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the reef knot's main weakness?", ["It is too bulky to use on bandages","It jams tight and cannot be undone","It can capsize if ropes are pulled hard","It cannot be tied with thick rope"], 2, "For joining ropes under strain, a sheet bend is more secure than a reef knot."],
    [1, "Why is the bowline so useful?", ["It tightens down hard on whatever it holds","It makes a fixed loop that stays easy to untie","It joins two ropes of different thickness","It stays secure even when left completely slack"], 1, "The bowline's loop stays fixed under load and can still be loosened afterwards."],
    [2, "Why use a round turn and two half hitches on a rope already under tension?", ["Friction from the wraps holds the strain","It is quicker to tie than a clove hitch","It suits only thin, slippery rope","It gives you a three-to-one pull"], 0, "Friction from the round turn holds the strain, giving you time to tie the hitches."],
    [3, "What does the trucker's hitch give you?", ["A simple pulley effect to tension a load","A knot that cannot be untied","A loop that never changes size","A stopper for the end of a line"], 0, "It multiplies your pull, so you can tension the rope more than you could by hand."],
    [4, "What is the best way to learn knots so they work when needed?", ["Read instructions until you remember them","Watch animations once and rely on memory","Learn as many knots as possible at once","Practise by hand until it is automatic"], 3, "Hands learn knots through repetition, so practise until it needs no thought."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The knot most people learn at school can fail when you need it most."});

WB.L("mending-clothes", "life", "beginner", "Mending clothes: buttons, seams and holes",
  "A missing button or a split seam sends a lot of good clothing to landfill. Ten minutes with a needle and thread can save it.",
  [
    ["Make do and mend", "Mending was once ordinary and is returning, and a small tin of supplies covers most repairs.",
      "During the Second World War, clothing in Britain was rationed, and in 1943 the government published a booklet called Make Do and Mend. It taught people to darn socks, patch elbows and make clothes last, as people always had. Today clothing is cheap and huge amounts end up in landfill, often for faults that take minutes to fix. Mending is returning to save money, reduce waste and, for many people, to calm the mind.\n\nA useful kit fits in a small tin: hand-sewing needles, thread in black, white, navy, grey and beige, pins, small sharp scissors, safety pins and spare buttons. A seam ripper makes unpicking easy. If unsure of a colour, choose slightly darker thread, which shows less."],
    ["Thread and buttons", "Use doubled thread with a secure knot, and leave slack under a button so it sits neatly.",
      "Cut thread about as long as your arm, as longer lengths tangle. For jobs that take strain, pull the thread through until the ends meet and knot them, so you sew with a double strand. Finish with a few small stitches in the same spot to lock it.\n\nTo sew on a button, start from the back so the knot is hidden. Go up through one hole and down the opposite one several times. Here is the trick most people miss: lay a toothpick or matchstick across the button and sew over it. Remove it, bring the needle up under the button and wind thread around the stitches. This forms a shank, so the button sits neatly without puckering."],
    ["Seams and hems", "Sew a split seam along its original stitch line, and use a slip stitch for hems so little shows.",
      "A split seam, perhaps under an arm, is often the quickest fix. Turn the garment inside out and pin the edges together along the old stitch line. Start a little before the gap, where the stitching is sound, and sew a backstitch: bring the needle up a short way ahead, then go back down at the end of the previous stitch. This makes a strong, continuous line. Carry on past the gap and lock off.\n\nFor a dropped hem, fold it back along its crease and pin it. With a slip stitch, hide the thread inside the fold and pick up just one or two threads of the main fabric each time. Keep stitches small and not too tight. Iron-on hemming tape is a quick fix that may not survive many washes."],
    ["Darning and visible mending", "Darning weaves new threads across a hole, and visible mending turns the repair into decoration.",
      "Darning is the traditional repair for socks and knitwear. Stretch the area over something rounded, such as a darning mushroom or a light bulb. Sew rows of thread across the hole, then weave more threads through them at right angles, creating a small patch of new fabric. For woven clothes like jeans, a patch on the inside or outside works well.\n\nMany menders now celebrate their repairs rather than hiding them. Sashiko, a Japanese stitching tradition whose name means roughly ‘little stabs’, uses rows of small running stitches, often white on indigo, to reinforce worn cloth. Patched and repatched Japanese textiles called boro, once a sign of poverty, are now admired as art."],
    ["Zips and knowing limits", "Many zip faults can be fixed cheaply, and part of the skill is knowing which jobs to pay for.",
      "Zips cause a lot of unnecessary clothing deaths. If a zip sticks, rub a graphite pencil, a bar of soap or a little candle wax along the teeth, then work the slider gently back and forth. If the teeth keep separating behind the slider, it has often widened with wear. Squeezing its sides very gently with pliers, a little at a time, can make it grip again.\n\nIf a pull tab snaps off, a small key ring or paperclip makes a serviceable replacement. Some repairs, such as replacing a whole zip in a coat, are fiddly enough that a tailor or alterations shop is worth the money. Knowing which jobs to do yourself and which to pay for is part of the skill."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is mending making a comeback?", ["Clothing has become far more expensive","Sewing is now taught in every workplace","Fashion brands now offer free mending","It saves money, cuts waste and is calming"], 3, "Cheap clothing fills landfill, and simple repairs offer savings, less waste and calm."],
    [1, "What does laying a toothpick across a button while sewing achieve?", ["It stops the thread from tangling","It keeps the button from ever coming off","It leaves slack so a shank can form","It hides the knot at the back"], 2, "The slack becomes a thread shank, letting the button sit on the buttonhole without puckering."],
    [2, "Where should you sew when repairing a split seam?", ["Across the fabric a few centimetres from the seam","Along the original stitch line, starting before the gap","Only on the outside with a loose stitch","Along a new line cut beside the old one"], 1, "Rejoining the original stitch line, from sound stitching, gives the strongest and neatest repair."],
    [3, "What is visible mending?", ["Hiding a hole with iron-on tape","Making the repair a feature, as in sashiko stitching","Using a darning mushroom to hide the patch","Replacing damaged clothing with new"], 1, "Visible mending celebrates repairs rather than hiding them, as in sashiko and boro."],
    [4, "What can often free a sticking zip?", ["Rubbing graphite or wax along the teeth","Cutting away the fabric that is stuck","Pulling the slider hard with pliers","Washing the garment in hot water"], 0, "A little lubricant on the teeth and a gentle wiggle fixes many zip problems."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Ten minutes with a needle can save a garment. Most people miss one button trick."});

WB.L("emergency-department-explained", "life", "beginner", "The emergency department: how it really works",
  "Why does someone who arrived after you get seen first? The answer goes back to Napoleon's battlefields, and understanding it makes a hospital visit far less frustrating.",
  [
    ["Born on the battlefield", "Triage means treating the sickest first, an idea that began in Napoleonic battlefield medicine.",
      "For much of history, wounded soldiers were treated according to rank, or not until the fighting ended. Dominique-Jean Larrey, a surgeon in Napoleon's armies, changed that. He introduced light, fast horse-drawn ‘flying ambulances’ to reach the wounded during battle, and insisted the most dangerously injured be treated first, whatever their rank or even their side.\n\nThe word triage comes from the French trier, meaning to sort. The idea spread through military medicine and, in the twentieth century, into civilian hospitals. The principle is the same today. An emergency department is not a queue. It is a sorting system designed to make sure the person most likely to die without help is seen first."],
    ["Triage and the scale", "A triage nurse decides how long you can safely wait, using five categories with maximum times to be seen.",
      "At an Australian emergency department you will usually speak first with a triage nurse. In a couple of minutes they ask what has happened, check key signs such as breathing and pulse, and look for danger. They are not diagnosing you. They are deciding how long you can safely wait. Be clear and specific, and neither exaggerate nor play things down.\n\nAustralian and New Zealand departments use the Australasian Triage Scale, in place in various forms since the 1990s. Category 1 is immediately life-threatening, seen immediately. Category 2, such as severe chest pain, within 10 minutes. Category 3 within 30 minutes, Category 4 within an hour and Category 5 within two hours. These are the maximum times for assessment and treatment to begin, not for the whole visit. Busy departments sometimes miss targets."],
    ["Why you might wait", "Long waits usually mean sicker people have arrived or the hospital's wards are full.",
      "Waiting rooms can be maddening, especially when others are called ahead of you. Usually someone more urgent has come in, perhaps by ambulance through a different door. Arriving by ambulance does not jump the queue. Ambulance patients are triaged too, and a less urgent one may wait in the same waiting room.\n\nAnother major cause of delay is access block. When patients who need admission cannot move to a ward because no beds are free, they stay in emergency, occupying cubicles and staff time. That is why waits often reflect pressure across the whole hospital rather than slow work in the department itself."],
    ["Choosing, and preparing", "Call 000 for emergencies, use a GP or urgent care for the rest, and bring your details if you go.",
      "Always call Triple Zero (000) or go to emergency for chest pain, difficulty breathing, signs of stroke such as a drooping face or slurred speech, severe bleeding, a serious head injury, seizures, severe burns, or someone unconscious or unresponsive. If in doubt, call 000. For a sprain, minor cut or ear infection, a GP, after-hours GP or urgent care clinic may see you faster, and pharmacists and nurse-staffed advice lines can help you decide.\n\nIf you go, bring your Medicare card, a list of medicines and allergies, and details of your doctor. Ask staff before you eat or drink, as you may need surgery or tests. A friend can listen and speak up for you. If your condition worsens while you wait, tell the triage nurse straight away. You can be reassessed and moved up."],
    ["Costs and afterwards", "Public emergency care is free for Medicare-eligible patients, though ambulance costs vary by state.",
      "If you are eligible for Medicare, treatment as a public patient in a public hospital emergency department costs nothing. Ambulances are different. Some states and territories cover their own residents, while others can charge substantial fees unless you have ambulance cover through a state scheme or private health insurance. Check your situation before you need one, though cost should never stop you calling 000 in an emergency.\n\nWhen you are discharged, ask for a copy of your discharge summary, make sure you understand any new medicines and warning signs, and book a follow-up with your GP. The emergency department fixes the crisis. Your GP handles what comes after."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What principle does triage follow?", ["The most seriously ill patient is seen first","Patients are seen in order of arrival","Patients are seen according to rank","Those who wait longest are seen first"], 0, "An emergency department is a sorting system, not a queue."],
    [1, "What is the triage nurse's job?", ["Diagnose your condition fully","Decide whether you pay","Book your follow-up with a GP","Decide how long you can safely wait"], 3, "Triage sets urgency, not a diagnosis, so give clear and honest information."],
    [2, "What is access block?", ["A delay caused by locked doors","A limit on visitors in treatment areas","Admitted patients waiting in emergency for a bed","Patients refused admission at the front desk"], 2, "Long waits often reflect pressure across the whole hospital, not slow work in emergency."],
    [3, "What should you do if your condition worsens while you wait?", ["Wait until your name is called","Leave and return later","Tell the triage nurse straight away","Ask another patient to check"], 2, "You can be reassessed and moved up, and staff would rather hear early."],
    [4, "Who pays for treatment in a public hospital emergency department?", ["You, unless you have private insurance","Nobody, for Medicare-eligible patients","Your GP, then you repay them","Your state ambulance service"], 1, "Public emergency care is free for Medicare-eligible patients, though ambulance fees vary."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why the person who arrived after you is seen first, and why that is fair."});

WB.L("making-small-talk", "life", "beginner", "Small talk that isn't so small",
  "Most of us avoid chatting with strangers because we expect it to be awkward. Experiments suggest we have badly misjudged both them and ourselves.",
  [
    ["We misjudge strangers", "We expect talking to strangers to be unpleasant and underestimate how much they like us, and we are wrong on both counts.",
      "In a 2014 study, psychologists Nicholas Epley and Juliana Schroeder asked Chicago train and bus commuters to talk to a stranger, keep to themselves or commute as usual. Beforehand, people predicted talking would be the least pleasant. Afterwards, those who chatted reported the most positive journeys. Those approached were generally happy to talk. The effects were modest, not life-changing.\n\nA 2018 study led by Erica Boothby found the liking gap: after chatting, people underestimated how much their partner liked them. We replay our own awkward moments, while the other person just found us pleasant. The gap appeared in labs, among university housemates and in public workshops. If you leave thinking you were boring, they very probably did not."],
    ["Why weak ties matter", "Brief friendly exchanges with acquaintances and strangers lift your mood and can open up opportunities.",
      "Close friends and family are central to wellbeing, but casual connections count too. Gillian Sandstrom and Elizabeth Dunn found that people felt happier on days with more interactions with weak ties, such as classmates, neighbours and the person who makes their coffee. In another study, customers asked to have a genuine, friendly exchange with a barista left in a better mood than those told to be efficient.\n\nWeak ties also bring practical benefits. Mark Granovetter's classic research suggested that new opportunities, including jobs, often come through acquaintances rather than close friends, because they move in different circles and hear about different things."],
    ["Openers and follow-ups", "Open with a shared situation, then ask follow-up questions, which make people like you more.",
      "Most people overthink the first sentence. You need a door, not wit. Comment on your shared situation: the weather, the queue, the food. ‘Have you been to one of these before?’ works at most gatherings. Open questions give the other person room to talk. A popular memory aid is FORD: family, occupation, recreation and dreams. Read the cues, though: someone with headphones on and eyes on a book may not want company.\n\nA 2017 Harvard study led by Karen Huang analysed thousands of conversations, including speed dating. People who asked more questions were better liked, and follow-up questions mattered most. If someone mentions Tasmania, ‘What was the highlight?’ shows you heard them, which switching to your own holiday does not. People tend to ask fewer questions than ideal."],
    ["Going a little deeper", "People enjoy meaningful conversations with strangers more than they expect to.",
      "Small talk is a warm-up, not a destination. Research by Michael Kardas, Amit Kumar and Nicholas Epley, published in 2022, found that people expected deep conversations with strangers to feel more awkward and less enjoyable than they actually were. Participants who answered more personal, meaningful questions consistently felt more connected than they had predicted.\n\nYou do not need to leap to intimate subjects. Moving from ‘What do you do?’ to ‘What do you enjoy most about it?’ or ‘How did you end up doing that?’ invites a real answer. Offering something genuine about yourself in return helps the other person feel safe going further."],
    ["Ending well", "Conversations rarely end when both people want them to, so a warm and clear exit is a kindness.",
      "Ending a conversation can feel harder than starting one. A 2021 study led by Adam Mastroianni asked people how long they had wanted their conversations to last. Conversations almost never ended when both people wanted them to. Often both wanted to stop earlier, or one wanted more and the other less, and neither could tell.\n\nSo a graceful exit helps. Summarise something from the chat, thank them and give a reason, even a simple one: ‘I'll let you get back to your evening. It was great hearing about the trip.’ If you would like to meet again, say so specifically. A clean, warm ending leaves both people with a good final impression."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the ‘liking gap’?", ["People like strangers less than friends","People underrate how much others like them","Conversations are shorter than expected","People overestimate how interesting they are"], 1, "We replay our awkward moments, while the other person usually found us perfectly pleasant."],
    [1, "Why can acquaintances be valuable for finding new opportunities?", ["They move in different social circles","They know us better than our close friends","They are far more likely to offer us a job","They give us more honest advice than family"], 0, "Weak ties connect us to information that our close circle does not have."],
    [2, "Which kind of question makes people like you more?", ["Questions that quickly move the subject to you","Yes-or-no questions that keep things brief","Questions about their income and work","Follow-up questions that build on their answer"], 3, "Follow-ups show you were listening, which is what most of us want from a conversation."],
    [3, "How do deeper conversations with strangers tend to go compared with expectations?", ["More awkward than people predicted","About as awkward as predicted","Shorter than people predicted","More enjoyable than people predicted"], 3, "People expected awkwardness but felt more connected after meaningful questions."],
    [4, "Why is a clear, warm exit a kindness?", ["People enjoy long goodbyes","Most people want conversations to last longer","Few end when both people want them to","Exits are only needed in business"], 2, "Neither person usually knows when the other wants to stop, so a clear exit helps both."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"We expect talking to strangers to be awkward. The evidence says otherwise."});

WB.L("jump-start-and-flat-tyre", "life", "intermediate", "Flat battery, flat tyre: getting yourself going",
  "The two most common reasons cars get stuck are a dead battery and a flat tyre. Both are fixable by the roadside, if you know the order of things and put safety first.",
  [
    ["Safety comes first", "Get well off the road, turn on your hazard lights, and only do the job yourself if it is genuinely safe.",
      "Before you touch a jack or a jumper lead, think about traffic, the biggest danger at any roadside breakdown. Pull off as far from moving cars as you can, onto firm, level ground if possible, and turn on your hazard lights. Put the handbrake on and switch off the engine.\n\nOn a busy highway or freeway, on a bend or in poor light, it is often safer to get out on the side away from traffic, stand well back behind a barrier if there is one, and call roadside assistance instead of attempting the job. If anyone is injured, there is a fire or fuel leak, or you are in danger, call Triple Zero (000)."],
    ["Jump-starting a car", "Connect red to positive on both cars, black to the good battery, and the last black clamp to bare metal away from the battery.",
      "A flat battery announces itself with rapid clicking, a slow laboured crank, or nothing, often with dim dashboard lights. Common causes are lights left on, short trips, weeks of disuse, age and Australian heat. If it is cracked, leaking, bulging or smells strongly of rotten eggs, do not jump it. Call for help.\n\nCheck both manuals. Park the cars close but not touching, with both engines off and both batteries 12 volts. Connect red to the flat battery's positive, the other red to the good battery's positive, then black to the good battery's negative. The last black clamp goes on bare, unpainted metal on the flat car's engine or body, away from the battery. Batteries can give off hydrogen gas, and this keeps any final spark away from it."],
    ["After the jump", "Run the donor car, start the flat one, remove leads in reverse, then drive and get the battery tested.",
      "Start the donor car and let it run for a few minutes to put charge into the flat battery. Then try the stranded car. If it does not start after a few short attempts, wait, as long cranking can overheat the starter motor. Once it runs, remove the leads in exactly the reverse order: black from the metal point, black from the good battery, red from the good battery, red from the formerly flat one. Never let clamps touch each other. Drive for a decent stretch, then have the battery tested soon.\n\nCompact lithium jump starter packs can start most petrol cars alone; keep one charged. Hybrids and EVs have a small 12-volt battery that powers the electronics. Manufacturers have specific procedures, so follow the manual."],
    ["Changing a flat tyre", "Loosen the wheel nuts while the wheel is on the ground, jack only at the marked point, and never go under the car.",
      "If a tyre is deflating, slow down gently and pull over as soon as it is safe. Driving on a flat ruins the tyre and can damage the wheel. Once safely stopped, find the spare, jack, wheel brace and locking wheel nut key. On a slope, put something heavy against the wheel diagonally opposite the flat. Before lifting, loosen each wheel nut about half a turn, usually anticlockwise, while the wheel can't spin.\n\nPlace the jack at the jacking point in the owner's manual and raise the car just until the tyre is clear. Never put any part of your body under a car held only by a jack. Fit the spare, snug the nuts by hand in a star pattern, lower the car, then tighten fully. Recheck the nuts after a short distance."],
    ["Space savers and kits", "A space-saver spare is for getting you to a tyre shop at reduced speed, not for everyday driving.",
      "Many cars carry a space-saver spare, a narrow temporary wheel that saves boot space and weight. It is designed to get you to a tyre shop, not for weeks of driving. Most carry a maximum speed label, often 80 kilometres per hour, and they reduce braking and cornering grip, so drive gently.\n\nSome newer cars have no spare, only a tyre repair kit with sealant and a small compressor. These can fix small punctures in the tread, such as from a nail, but not sidewall cuts or major damage. Find out what your car carries, and practise once at home. A calm driveway lesson beats a first attempt on a highway at night."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the biggest danger at a roadside breakdown?", ["Overheating tyres","A flat battery","Passing traffic","Running out of fuel"], 2, "Think about traffic first, and call roadside assistance if the location is unsafe."],
    [1, "Why does the final black clamp go on bare metal away from the battery?", ["To save time when connecting the last lead","To keep any spark away from battery hydrogen gas","Because battery terminals are always painted","To charge the car body as well as the battery"], 1, "Placing the last connection away from the battery avoids a spark near explosive gas."],
    [2, "What should you do after the car starts?", ["Remove leads in reverse, drive a while, test the battery","Switch the engine off straight away to rest it","Leave the leads attached until the morning","Reconnect the leads to double-check the charge"], 0, "A battery that went flat once may be on its way out, so test it soon."],
    [3, "Why loosen the wheel nuts before lifting the car?", ["The wheel cannot spin while it is on the ground","The jack can then lift the car faster","The spare then fits onto the studs more easily","It lets hot brakes cool before lifting"], 0, "With the tyre on the ground, you can apply force without the wheel turning."],
    [4, "What is a space-saver spare designed for?", ["Everyday driving for several weeks or months","Towing a trailer on a long highway trip","Replacing all four tyres on a long journey","Getting you to a tyre shop at reduced speed"], 3, "It is a temporary wheel with reduced grip, so drive gently and replace it promptly."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Which cable goes on last matters more than you'd think. Here's why."});

WB.L("map-and-compass", "life", "intermediate", "Reading a map and compass",
  "Phones die, satellites drop out and screens are hard to read in the rain. A paper map and a compass need no battery, and the skills to use them are surprisingly learnable.",
  [
    ["Paper and scale", "A paper map and compass never run out of battery, and a map's scale tells you real distances.",
      "Phone navigation is brilliant until the battery goes flat in the cold, the screen cracks or you lose signal in a deep valley. Bushwalking clubs and search and rescue groups in Australia still advise carrying a paper map and compass, and knowing how to use them, as a backup. Navigating with a map also makes you read the land and notice ridges and creeks. People following a blue dot often have little idea of their surroundings.\n\nWalking maps are usually topographic, with scales such as 1:25,000 or 1:50,000. On a 1:25,000 map, one centimetre is 250 metres; on a 1:50,000 map, 500 metres. The larger the second number, the more ground the map covers and the less detail. Map distance is flat, so a steep kilometre takes longer than a level one."],
    ["Contours and grid references", "Contour lines reveal the shape of the land, and grid references give a location with eastings first, then northings.",
      "Each contour line joins points of the same height, and the contour interval is in the legend. Close lines mean steep ground; wide spacing means gentle ground. Stacked closed rings mark a hill or peak. V or U shapes pointing uphill show a valley or gully, often with a creek. Pointing downhill, they show a spur, a ridge running down from higher ground.\n\nA grid reference describes a location precisely, perhaps to rescuers. Australian topographic maps use the Map Grid of Australia, with vertical lines called eastings and horizontal lines called northings. Give the easting first, then the northing: ‘along the corridor, then up the stairs’. A four-figure reference identifies a grid square, usually one kilometre across. Six figures estimate tenths within it, to about 100 metres."],
    ["Norths and bearings", "Compasses point to magnetic north, so you must correct a map bearing for declination.",
      "True north is the geographic North Pole. Grid north follows the map's vertical grid lines and is very close to true north. Magnetic north is where your compass points. The difference is magnetic declination. In Australia it ranges from near zero in parts of Western Australia to over ten degrees east in the south-east. Maps print the declination. A ten-degree error over five kilometres puts you almost a kilometre off course.\n\nTo take a bearing, lay a baseplate compass's edge on the map joining where you are to where you are going, with its arrow pointing to the destination. Turn the dial until the orienting lines parallel the grid lines, with the orienting arrow to the top of the map. Read the bearing, then correct for declination: where it is east, subtract it."],
    ["Walking on a bearing", "Turn your body until the red needle sits in the orienting arrow, then walk from landmark to landmark.",
      "Hold the compass flat with the direction-of-travel arrow pointing ahead. Turn your whole body until the red, north end of the needle sits inside the orienting arrow, often remembered as ‘red in the shed’. The arrow now points along your bearing. Rather than staring at the compass, pick a distinct landmark in that direction, walk to it, then repeat.\n\nKeep the compass away from metal, phones, car bodies and power lines, which can pull the needle. Another technique is aiming off: when heading for a point on a creek or track, deliberately aim to one side so you know which way to turn on arrival."],
    ["A compass for here", "Compasses are balanced for different zones, so use one suited to Australia and practise somewhere safe first.",
      "Earth's magnetic field also dips downward at an angle that changes with latitude. A compass needle is weighted to cancel that dip in the region where it will be used. A compass balanced for Europe or North America may tilt, drag on its housing and read sluggishly in Australia. Check it is balanced for the southern hemisphere or Australian zone, or choose a global needle.\n\nPractise somewhere safe and familiar, such as a local park or well-marked track, before relying on your skills in remote country. And always tell someone your plans and carry an emergency beacon where there is no phone coverage."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "On a 1:50,000 map, what does one centimetre represent?", ["50 metres","250 metres","5 kilometres","500 metres"], 3, "One unit on the map equals 50,000 on the ground, so a centimetre is 500 metres."],
    [1, "What do closely spaced contour lines show?", ["Gentle ground","A flat plain","Steep ground","A walking track"], 2, "Close contours mean the height changes quickly over a short distance."],
    [2, "Why must you correct for magnetic declination?", ["The map's grid lines are printed wrongly each year","Your compass points to magnetic north, not grid north","Compasses read too slowly on a steep slope","True north moves a few degrees every year"], 1, "Without correction, a ten-degree error over five kilometres puts you almost a kilometre off course."],
    [3, "What is the point of aiming off?", ["It saves distance by cutting across a corner","You know which way to turn on reaching a creek","It avoids magnetic interference from metal objects","It lets you skip choosing landmarks to walk to"], 1, "Deliberately aiming to one side removes the guesswork when you arrive at a linear feature."],
    [4, "Why should a compass match your region?", ["Earth's magnetic field dips by latitude, so needles drag","Compasses only work in a single hemisphere","Mismatched compasses always read true north","Locally made compasses are always much cheaper"], 0, "Needles are weighted for a magnetic zone, so choose one balanced for Australia or a global model."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your compass needle doesn't point to true north. Here's the gap and why it matters."});

WB.L("hiring-a-tradie", "life", "intermediate", "Hiring a tradie without regrets",
  "Most tradies are honest, skilled and busy. A few simple checks help you find them, and avoid the small number who leave a trail of half-finished jobs.",
  [
    ["Licence and recommendations", "Check a tradie's licence on the official register and choose people through trusted recommendations.",
      "In every Australian state and territory, electrical work must be done by a licensed electrician, and gas fitting and most plumbing by licensed tradespeople. Building work above a certain value also generally requires a licensed builder. Faulty wiring, gas leaks and structural mistakes can kill. Each state's regulator runs a free online register where you can check a licence is current, what it covers and any disciplinary actions. Ask for the number and check it yourself. Also confirm public liability insurance.\n\nThe best leads come from neighbours, friends and family who had similar work recently. Choose detailed reviews and be wary of a burst of short, glowing ones. Ask about similar jobs, who will do the work and whether subcontractors are involved. Someone vague, who never returns calls or will not put things in writing is unlikely to change."],
    ["Quotes, contracts and deposits", "Compare written quotes for the same scope, and expect a written contract and limited deposit on bigger jobs.",
      "For more than a small job, get at least three written quotes. A quote sets out the work, materials, total price, whether GST is included and how long it is valid. An estimate is only a best guess, and the bill can legitimately end up higher. Make sure every quote covers the same scope. The cheapest may leave out rubbish removal or quality materials. Agree changes in writing, with the price, before they are done.\n\nAbove certain values, a written contract is generally required, setting out the work, price, timeframes and dispute handling. Read it before signing. Several states cap the deposit a builder can ask for on domestic work, commonly at around 10 per cent or less. For bigger projects, tie payments to completed stages, not calendar dates, which matters if the business collapses partway."],
    ["Insurance that protects you", "Home warranty insurance is a last-resort safety net if the builder dies, disappears or goes broke.",
      "Most states require builders to take out home warranty insurance, known by different names, on residential jobs above a set value. It typically covers you if the builder dies, disappears or becomes insolvent and cannot finish the work or fix defects. The builder must usually give you the certificate before taking a deposit or starting work, so ask to see it.\n\nSeparately, tell your own home and contents insurer about major renovations, as some policies change cover while building work is under way. The tradie's public liability insurance covers damage they cause to your property or injuries to other people, so check that it is current too."],
    ["Red flags", "Door knockers after storms, pressure to decide now and demands for big cash payments are warning signs.",
      "Be very wary of anyone who knocks uninvited, especially after a storm or flood, claiming your roof or gutters urgently need work. Under consumer law, agreements made after an uninvited approach generally come with a cooling-off period of 10 business days.\n\nOther red flags include pressure to sign today, a quote far below everyone else's, no ABN, no fixed address, refusal to give a written quote and a request for most of the money up front, especially in cash. Cash with no invoice leaves you with no proof of what was agreed. Pay by traceable methods, keep every receipt and never hand over the full amount before the job is finished."],
    ["When things go wrong", "Raise problems early and in writing, then escalate to your state's fair trading or building regulator if needed.",
      "Under the Australian Consumer Law, services must be provided with due care and skill, be fit for any purpose you made clear, and be delivered within a reasonable time if no deadline was agreed. If something is not right, raise it calmly and promptly, ideally while the tradie is on site, then follow up in writing with photos. Give them a fair chance to fix it, because most will.\n\nIf they will not, contact your state's fair trading office or building regulator, which can often help resolve disputes. Some states run building dispute services, and civil tribunals can decide claims. Keep a folder of quotes, contracts, messages, invoices and photos from the start. Good records are your strongest tool."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How should you check a tradie's licence?", ["Look it up on your state regulator's free register","Take their word because they sound confident","Ask a neighbour whether the card looks real","Check only that they hold liability insurance"], 0, "The register shows whether a licence is current, what it covers and any disciplinary actions."],
    [1, "What is the difference between a quote and an estimate?", ["A quote is always cheaper than an estimate","An estimate is legally binding on the tradie","A quote never includes the cost of materials","A quote is a firm price; an estimate is a best guess"], 3, "With only an estimate, the final bill can legitimately end up higher."],
    [2, "What does home warranty insurance protect you against?", ["Damage you cause to your own property","Injuries to the tradie while on site","The builder dying, disappearing or going broke","Rising material prices during the job"], 2, "It is a last-resort safety net, and you should ask to see the certificate before work starts."],
    [3, "What generally applies when you agree to work after an uninvited approach?", ["No right to cancel","A full refund after any delay","A cooling-off period of 10 business days","A mandatory cash deposit"], 2, "Consumer law gives you time to reconsider an agreement made after an uninvited approach."],
    [4, "What should you do first if a job is not right?", ["Withhold payment without telling the tradie","Raise it promptly, then follow up in writing","Post an online review before speaking to them","Wait until the whole job is finished"], 1, "Early, documented communication gives them a fair chance to fix it and builds your records."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Before you sign anything, there is a free check most people skip."});

WB.L("after-someone-dies", "life", "intermediate", "When someone dies: the practical steps",
  "In the middle of grief come phone calls, forms and decisions nobody prepares you for. Knowing the order of things can lift some of the weight.",
  [
    ["First hours and certification", "Call 000 for a sudden death, but an expected death needs no rush, and some deaths must go to the coroner.",
      "If someone dies suddenly or unexpectedly, or you are unsure whether they have died, call Triple Zero (000) straight away. Operators will guide you, and paramedics and often police will attend. If the death was expected, for example someone in palliative care at home, there is no need for an ambulance. Contact their doctor or palliative care team, who will arrange for the death to be confirmed. There is generally no requirement to have the body taken away immediately, so you can take time to sit with the person and call family.\n\nBefore a funeral, a doctor must complete a medical certificate stating the cause of death. Sudden, unexpected, violent, accidental or unexplained deaths must be reported to the coroner, who may order a post-mortem or an inquest. Coronial staff can explain each step."],
    ["The funeral and registration", "The funeral director usually registers the death, and a meaningful farewell need not be expensive.",
      "Most families engage a funeral director, who collects the person, helps arrange the service and usually lodges the details to register the death with the state or territory Registry of Births, Deaths and Marriages. The registry then issues the official death certificate, which differs from the doctor's medical certificate. You will need it, or certified copies, for banks, super funds and insurers.\n\nCheck whether the person left wishes about burial, cremation or music. A funeral can be religious or secular, large or small. Funerals can cost many thousands of dollars, and grieving families may overspend out of love or guilt. Get more than one quote, ask for itemised prices and consider simpler options such as direct cremation followed by a memorial gathering. A calmer friend can join the meetings."],
    ["Telling organisations", "Many organisations need to know, and a free national service can notify several at once.",
      "A surprising number of organisations need to be told: Services Australia for Centrelink and Medicare, the Australian Taxation Office, banks, super funds, insurers, employers, utility and phone companies, and more. The free Australian Death Notification Service, run with the state registries, lets you notify a number of participating organisations in one go once the death is registered.\n\nKeep a list of who you have contacted, when and what they said. Many banks and large companies have dedicated bereavement teams who can make it gentler. Redirecting mail and closing unused accounts also helps protect against criminals who misuse the identities of people who have died."],
    ["Money and the estate", "The executor manages the estate, so do not distribute belongings until it is clear who has legal authority.",
      "If there is a will, the executor it names manages the estate: gathering assets, paying debts and distributing what remains. If there is no will, a close relative can apply to the court to be appointed administrator. Until it is clear who has the authority, avoid giving away belongings or money, however obvious the wishes seem.\n\nSome things move outside the estate. Jointly held bank accounts and property usually pass straight to the surviving owner, and super is paid by the fund's trustee, often following the member's nominations. Many banks will pay funeral invoices directly from the deceased's account. A final tax return is often needed, and solicitors and public trustees can help."],
    ["Looking after the living", "Very little must happen immediately, so accept help, take things in order and be kind to yourself.",
      "In the first weeks the to-do list can feel endless, but very little has to happen immediately. Prioritise the essentials, such as the funeral and anything protecting the estate, and let the rest wait. Accept practical offers such as cooking, making calls or minding children, and consider putting one organised friend or relative in charge of the paperwork list.\n\nGrief comes in waves and can bring exhaustion, poor sleep and forgetfulness. Talk with your GP if you are struggling, and know that grief support services and helplines exist. If you are ever worried about your safety or someone else's, call 000. Being kind to yourself is part of the work."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What should you do first if a death is sudden or unexpected?", ["Contact a funeral director","Call Triple Zero (000)","Wait for a doctor's visit next day","Notify the bank"], 1, "Emergency operators guide you, and paramedics and often police attend."],
    [1, "How does the official death certificate differ from the doctor's medical certificate?", ["It is issued by the state registry","It is signed by the funeral director","It states the cause of death only","It is needed before the doctor certifies"], 0, "You need the registry's death certificate or certified copies to deal with banks, super funds and insurers."],
    [2, "What does the Australian Death Notification Service do?", ["Arranges and pays for the funeral service","Pays the deceased's remaining debts","Appoints the executor named in a will","Lets you notify several organisations at once"], 3, "It is free and saves repeating the same notification to many organisations."],
    [3, "Why avoid giving away belongings early?", ["The belongings must all be sold first","Beneficiaries must be paid only in cash","The funeral director owns them temporarily","It may not yet be clear who has legal authority"], 3, "Only the executor or administrator has authority to distribute the estate."],
    [4, "What is a sound approach to the paperwork in the first weeks?", ["Finish every task within the first week","Handle it all alone to avoid burdening others","Do the essentials, accept help, let the rest wait","Put it aside entirely for a year"], 2, "Very little has to happen immediately, and help and rest are part of coping."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Most of what must happen after a death can wait. Here is what cannot."});

WB.L("buying-a-home-step-by-step", "life", "advanced", "Buying a home: from inspection to settlement",
  "Buying a home is the biggest purchase most people ever make, and the process is full of unfamiliar words and quiet traps. Here is the path from first inspection to handing over the keys.",
  [
    ["Know your true budget", "Pre-approval is only an indication of what a lender may offer, and the costs on top of the price add up quickly.",
      "Find out what you can spend before you fall for a house. Pre-approval, sometimes called conditional approval, is a lender's indication of how much it may lend. It is not a guarantee: the lender still assesses the property and your circumstances.\n\nThen add the costs on top of the price. Stamp duty (transfer duty) is often the biggest. Others include conveyancing, inspections, loan fees, possibly lenders mortgage insurance, moving and repairs.\n\nFirst home buyers can get help. Most states offer duty concessions or exemptions, and federal guarantees have let eligible buyers purchase with a five per cent deposit and no lenders mortgage insurance. The First Home Super Saver Scheme, running since 2017, lets you save a deposit through voluntary super contributions, within limits. Rules change often, so check current ones."],
    ["Inspect before you commit", "A building and pest inspection, plus a strata report for units, can reveal problems you cannot see on an open-home visit.",
      "A home that looks lovely at an open inspection can hide serious problems. A professional building inspection checks for structural defects, roof damage, rising damp, cracking and safety issues. Termites are a real threat in much of Australia, so a pest inspection is usually done at the same time.\n\nFor an apartment or townhouse in a strata scheme, a strata report matters just as much. It shows the owners corporation's finances, including savings for future major works, any special levies charged or planned, building defects, disputes and the by-laws, which may restrict pets, renovations or short-term letting.\n\nAn inspection costs a few hundred dollars. Missing a structural problem can cost tens of thousands."],
    ["Contract and sale type", "Have a conveyancer or solicitor review the contract before you sign, and know that auctions are unconditional while private sales may not be.",
      "Conveyancing transfers ownership from seller to buyer. Hire a licensed conveyancer or solicitor before you make an offer, not after. They review the contract of sale and the seller's disclosure documents, which vary by state. They look for easements, which give others rights over part of the land, and covenants, which can restrict what you build. They also check special conditions that may favour the seller.\n\nIn a private sale, you can make an offer subject to conditions such as finance approval or a satisfactory inspection. Most states also allow a short cooling-off period, often a few business days, in which you can withdraw by paying a small fee. At auction there is generally no cooling-off period, and the winning bidder is committed on the spot. Finance, inspections and legal review must be finished before auction day."],
    ["Exchange to settlement", "Once contracts are exchanged you are legally bound and pay a deposit, so use the weeks before settlement to finalise the loan, insurance and a final inspection.",
      "When both sides sign the contract, and in many states exchange copies, the sale becomes legally binding, subject to any cooling-off period or conditions. You then pay a deposit, commonly 10 per cent, though a smaller one can sometimes be negotiated, or a deposit bond used. It is usually held in trust. If you fail to complete without a valid reason, you can lose it and face further costs.\n\nSettlement is commonly 30 to 90 days later. Your lender finalises the loan, and your conveyancer completes searches and adjusts council rates and water charges. In some states the property is at your risk from the contract date, so ask when to arrange building insurance. A final inspection is usually allowed before settlement. Avoid new loans or job changes, which can complicate final approval."],
    ["Settlement day", "Settlement now mostly happens electronically, after which the title is yours and the agent releases the keys.",
      "On settlement day you usually do not need to be anywhere special. Settlement in Australia now mostly happens on electronic platforms, where the lawyers, conveyancers and banks for both sides complete the exchange of money and documents online. Funds move, the title is transferred into your name and the seller's loan is paid out.\n\nYour conveyancer confirms when it is done, and the agent then releases the keys. Switch on utilities in your name, update your address and check your insurance is active.\n\nKeep the settlement statement and contract with your important records. They matter when you eventually sell, and for capital gains tax if the property is ever rented out or is no longer your main residence."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does pre-approval from a lender tell you?", ["About how much a lender may lend","The exact loan you will receive","That the property has passed valuation","That stamp duty will be waived"], 0, "Pre-approval is an indication only; the lender still assesses the property and your circumstances."],
    [1, "Why is a pest inspection usually done with a building inspection?", ["It is required by law before every sale","It replaces the need for a strata report","Pests are visible at any open home","Termites are a real threat in much of Australia"], 3, "Termites can cause serious hidden damage, so a pest check is usually done alongside the building inspection."],
    [2, "What is the key difference between buying at auction and by private sale?", ["Auction buyers always get a longer cooling-off period","Auction wins are generally unconditional with no cooling-off","Private sales cannot include conditions","Auctions do not need a contract"], 1, "Because an auction win is binding on the spot, finance, inspections and legal checks must come first."],
    [3, "Why should you ask about building insurance soon after signing?", ["Insurance is only needed after you move in","The lender pays for it until settlement","In some states, risk passes to you at contract","Insurance is included in the deposit"], 2, "Risk can pass to the buyer at contract, so insurance should not wait until you move in."],
    [4, "Why keep your settlement statement and contract afterwards?", ["The agent requires them each year","They prove your loan rate to the bank","They record your costs, which matter for tax","They must be lodged with council rates"], 2, "Records of what you paid help with tax calculations, including capital gains tax if the property is ever rented out or is not your main residence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your offer is not the real price of a house. Here is what else you pay."});

